===persian===

چه شبی بود آن شب...
رفته بودی تو... و...
بوی خوشِ تو...
در دلم دانهٔ حسرت می‌کاشت...
یادِ دستانِ تو بر پیکر من
سنگ از چشمهٔ اشکم بر می‌داشت...
چه شبی بود آن شب...
رفتی و یادِ تو ماند...
کاش می‌دانستی...
تا سحر با یادت...
وصل می‌زد... هجران می‌خواند...
زمستان ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

What a night that night was...
You had gone... and...
your sweet scent...
was planting the seed of regret in my heart...
The memory of your hands on my body
was lifting the stone from the spring of my tears...
What a night that night was...
You went, and the memory of you stayed...
I wish you had known...
until dawn, with your memory...
union played... parting sang...
Winter 1368

===lantern===

What a night that night was...
You had gone... and...
your sweet scent
was sowing the seed of regret in my heart...
The memory of your hands on my body
lifted the stone off the spring of my tears...
What a night that night was...
You went, and what stayed was the memory of you...
If only you had known —
how, till dawn, with your memory,
union played... and parting sang...

Winter 1368

===translation===

===footnotes===

شب یلدا, Yalda, is the winter solstice: the longest night of the year, kept awake
in company with poetry and pomegranates. A Yalda that is هجرانی — a Yalda of separation — is the
longest night there is, spent apart, and the title says so before the poem starts.

The last line makes a duet out of the two states the whole ghazal tradition runs on. وصل, union,
and هجران, parting, are given a verb each: union strikes the strings (می‌زد) and parting sings
(می‌خواند). One plays, the other sings, and the poet lies awake between them until morning.

